      <section class="paper-section reader-explanation" id="explanation" aria-labelledby="explanation-heading">
        <h2 id="explanation-heading">Reader’s explanation</h2>
      <section class="paper-section reader-note" aria-labelledby="amplifier-noise-heading">
        <h3 id="amplifier-noise-heading">What an amplifier cannot leave behind</h3>
        <p class="reader-lede">A thermal calculation is easy. The question is whether any independent quantum inputs with the same entropies can do better.</p>
        <p>Feed vacuum into both ports of an amplifier with gain <var>G</var> &gt; 1. The output you keep is thermal, with <var>G</var> − 1 photons per mode. The pump supplies the energy. The unavoidable extra contribution comes from the quantum commutation relation.</p>
        <p>Simply multiplying a mode’s annihilation operator by √<var>G</var> would multiply its commutator by <var>G</var>. A physical amplifier needs a second mode, entering through a creation operator, to restore the correct commutator. That reversal brings in the identity <var>bb</var><sup>†</sup> = <var>b</var><sup>†</sup><var>b</var> + 1. The extra unit survives even when that second input is vacuum.</p>
        <p>The amplifier is unitary on its two outputs together: it preserves their joint entropy. The thermal entropy appears in the retained output because the other output is discarded. The distinction between a pure joint state and a mixed marginal is essential.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="paper-section reader-note" aria-labelledby="sharp-bound-heading">
        <h3 id="sharp-bound-heading">Put entropy on a thermal scale</h3>
        <p>A one-mode thermal state with mean occupation <var>r</var> has entropy <var>g</var>(<var>r</var>) = (<var>r</var> + 1) log(<var>r</var> + 1) − <var>r</var> log <var>r</var>, with <var>g</var>(0) = 0. All logarithms here are natural.</p>
        <p>For an <var>n</var>-mode state, ask which thermal occupation would give the same entropy per mode. The answer is its <em>entropy photon number</em>, <span class="inline-math">𝒩<sub>n</sub>(ρ) = <var>g</var><sup>−1</sup>(<var>S</var>(ρ)/<var>n</var>)</span>. This measures uncertainty on a thermal scale; it is not the state’s actual mean energy.</p>
        <p>Write Γ<sub>G</sub>(ρ<sub>A</sub>, ρ<sub>B</sub>) for the retained amplifier output. The manuscript proposes the sharp inequality</p>
        <div class="equation" tabindex="0" role="math" aria-label="The entropy photon number of the amplifier output is at least G times that of input A plus G minus one times the entropy photon number of input B plus one.">
          <math display="block">
            <mrow><msub><mi>𝒩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>𝒩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝒩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
          </math>
        </div>
        <p>The claim concerns every fixed finite number of modes and finite-energy inputs. The two input groups must be independent, although the modes within either group may be correlated or entangled. Thermal inputs attain equality: their occupations combine as <var>Ga</var> + (<var>G</var> − 1)(<var>b</var> + 1).</p>
        <p>For example, at gain 2, thermal inputs with occupations 1 and 0 give output occupation 3. The proposed bound says that replacing those inputs by arbitrary independent states with the same entropies cannot lower the output’s entropy photon number below 3.</p>
        <p>Independence cannot be weakened to separability. Section 9 gives classically correlated coherent inputs whose displacements cancel at the output and defeat the independent-input formula.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="paper-section reader-note" id="two-signs" aria-labelledby="two-signs-heading">
        <h3 id="two-signs-heading">Where the two signs meet</h3>
        <p>The proposed proof approaches the bound from two directions. One is geometric: the curvature of entropy gives a comparison involving logarithmic means. Four-weight interpolation turns that comparison into a thermal metric. The creation port reverses the order of the metric’s two thermal rates.</p>
        <p>The other direction is dynamical. Relax each input toward a thermal reference. For the amplifier to carry these relaxations into the matching output relaxation, the reference occupations must obey the same signed balance, <var>R</var> = <var>Ga</var> + (<var>G</var> − 1)(<var>b</var> + 1). The extra unit and the reversed orientation have appeared again.</p>
        <p>The point is not merely that the formulas resemble one another. At a regularized minimum of a putative entropy deficit, the two structures are paired in the stationary equations. Their energy corrections cancel exactly. Equation (6.5) is the meeting point: interpolation bounds the remaining output defect, while strict comparison slack gives the input defects a larger opposing coefficient.</p>
        <p>The argument then forces those defects to vanish as the moment penalty is removed. A thermal Stein estimate turns small balance residuals into closeness to the thermal states in trace norm. Uniform energy bounds supply the final entropy-continuity step. The minimizing states return to the thermal calculation, contradicting the negative deficit from which the argument began.</p>
        <p>This agreement between entropy geometry and thermal dynamics is the organizing idea of the proof candidate. <a href="{{PDF}}">Sections 3–7 and Appendices A–D give the complete argument and its domain estimates.</a></p>
      </section>

      <section class="paper-section" id="contribution" aria-labelledby="contribution-heading">
        <h3 id="contribution-heading">Contribution and prior work</h3>
        <p>The inequality is the amplifier conjecture of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/nphoton.2014.252">De Palma, Mari and Giovannetti</a>. The manuscript’s claim is a proof for two arbitrary independent finite-energy multimode inputs, extending beyond <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.118.160503">one-mode Gaussian-channel optimizer results</a> and restricted multimode cases.</p>
        <p>The regularized variational framework and four-weight interpolation are adapted from <a href="{{UPSTREAM}}">OpenAI’s beam-splitter proof</a>. The active-port analysis, its matching relaxation balance, stationary cancellation and finite-energy closure are the amplifier-specific parts presented here. The full adapted interpolation argument is retained in Appendix C.</p>
        <p>The Gaussian-input inequality itself is established prior work. Section 8 gives a spectral refinement and a separate equality argument using <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/1.4935852">Bhatia–Jain’s symplectic variational principle</a>. Within the Gaussian class, equality requires flat symplectic spectra in each input group and a common normalized covariance shape after reversing the second input’s momentum quadratures. Displacements remain free.</p>
        <p>The trace-norm Stein estimate in Section 7 builds on <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfa.2024.110336">Gao–Rouzé’s thermal beam-splitter smoothing mechanism</a>. Section 9 derives a sharp two-output correlation bound conditional on the general amplifier inequality. General non-Gaussian equality and a quantitative EPnI stability estimate remain open; the Stein estimate alone does not settle them. Priority for the refinements is not established by this release.</p>
      </section>
      </section>
